---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PatchBench from "../../components/exhibits/PatchBench.astro";
import ExhibitExplorer from "../../components/exhibits/ExhibitExplorer.astro";
export const prerender = false;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The patch bench"
  description="A working exhibit: play a small game, hear its own sound effects, then pull the music apart from both. The real LoftwahFM client, the real separation."
  path="/exhibits/patch-bench/"
  currentPath="/exhibits/"
  image="/og/woodhouse-workshop-20261018.jpg"
  imageAlt="An illustrated maker at a workbench connects a miniature underwater world, game arena and record player."
>
  <section class="container exhibit-intro">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">WOODHOUSE</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <a href="/factory/">Factory</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span>The patch bench</span>
    </nav>
    <div class="exhibit-intro__grid">
      <div>
        <p class="micro-label">Working exhibit · Woodhouse</p>
        <h1 class="page-title">
          Your game.
          <br />
          <em>Its own soundtrack.</em>
        </h1>
      </div>
      <div class="exhibit-intro__aside">
        <p>
          This is not a picture of a demo. It is the demo, running here, with the parts separated so
          you can see what actually owns what.
        </p>
        <p>
          Play the run, fire its effects, switch on a real LoftwahFM station, then pull the music
          link out and watch exactly what stops — and what does not.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container exhibit-stage">
    <PatchBench
      variant="full"
      recipeHref="/dispatches/your-game-its-own-soundtrack/"
      recipeLabel="Read the story behind it"
    />
  </section>

  <section class="container exhibit-explorer-band">
    <ExhibitExplorer />
  </section>

  <section class="container exhibit-body">
    <div class="exhibit-body__grid">
      <div class="exhibit-prose">
        <h2>Why the parts are separate</h2>
        <p>
          A game that wants a soundtrack has three jobs to do, and they belong to three different
          owners. The game runs the world and decides when something happens. The effects belong to
          the game and are generated by it. The music belongs to a music service, arrives over a
          network, and can fail on its own schedule.
        </p>
        <p>
          The temptation is to wire them into one audio system and let one thing carry the lot. It
          works right up until the music service is slow, the manifest is wrong, or the stream fails
          to open. Then the whole thing goes quiet together and nobody can tell which part broke.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the bench, the three owners are kept apart on purpose. The effects run on a bus the
          game owns. The music runs on a bus the page owns, carrying a stream from LoftwahFM's
          versioned browser client. When the run clips a gate, it ducks the music by moving one gain
          — it never pauses it, never restarts it, and never resumes music the visitor paused.
        </p>
        <h2>What to take from it</h2>
        <p>If you are building a browser game, this is the shape worth copying:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>One audio owner.</strong> Extend the audio manager your game already has.
            Effects, dialogue and local music stay where they are.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Music is optional.</strong> If the client, the station manifest or the media
            fail, the game reports it quietly and keeps running.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Off means off.</strong> Start muted, load the visitor's saved preference before
            you unlock audio, and treat an explicit Play as the only thing that starts sound.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Duck with gain.</strong> Lowering a bus is not the same as pausing a stream. The
            distinction is why a bass hit never restarts the music.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Release it.</strong> Suspend on a hidden tab, stay quiet on return, and tear the
            client down with the audio owner that created it.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          LoftwahFM publishes the client, the public stations and a short recipe for this. The
          recipe is the part you will actually want.
        </p>
        <p class="exhibit-prose__actions">
          <a class="home-action" href="https://fm.loftwah.com/game-music">
            Get the integration recipe <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
          <a class="text-link" href="/lab/game-music.html">
            Try the plain standalone version <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="exhibit-margin" aria-label="About this exhibit">
        <div class="exhibit-margin__block">
          <span class="micro-label">What you are looking at</span>
          <p>
            A real page on this site. The game runs locally, the effects are generated by
            oscillators, and the soundtrack is a public LoftwahFM station streamed from a different
            origin.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="exhibit-margin__block">
          <span class="micro-label">What it does not claim</span>
          <p>
            No customer adopted this. No audio is bundled or relicensed. The station list is
            LoftwahFM’s own published catalogue, and a missing stream is reported as missing.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="exhibit-margin__block">
          <span class="micro-label">Reviewed</span>
          <p>20 October 2026. The client is pinned to LoftwahFM browser client 1.2.0.</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .exhibit-intro {
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
  }
  .exhibit-intro__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.35fr 1fr;
    gap: 2.5rem 4rem;
    align-items: end;
    padding-block: 1.5rem 2rem;
  }
  .exhibit-intro .page-title {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
  }
  .exhibit-intro__aside p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    max-width: 46ch;
    color: var(--copy);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .exhibit-stage {
    padding-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .exhibit-body {
    padding-block: 2.5rem 4rem;
  }
  .exhibit-body__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.6fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 3rem 4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding-top: 2rem;
  }
  .exhibit-prose h2 {
    margin: 0 0 0.8rem;
    font: 700 clamp(1.5rem, 2.6vw, 2rem)/1.15 var(--display);
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }
  .exhibit-prose h2 + p {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .exhibit-prose p {
    max-width: 66ch;
    color: var(--copy);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .exhibit-prose ul {
    max-width: 66ch;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--copy);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .exhibit-prose li {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .exhibit-prose strong {
    color: var(--paper);
  }
  .exhibit-prose__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .exhibit-margin {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  .exhibit-margin__block {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding-top: 0.85rem;
  }
  .exhibit-margin__block p {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--copy-soft);
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .exhibit-intro__grid,
    .exhibit-body__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 1.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
